<section id="joins" class="band" aria-labelledby="joins-title">
  <h2 id="joins-title">How lines join</h2>
  <p>Every way the shelf joins two things, with the word spwashi.com's slot reader gives each and the name the workbench gives its mark.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table class="joins">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">form, and where</th><th scope="col">reader says</th><th scope="col">mark is called</th><th scope="col" class="n">lines</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>a <span data-t="potential">~</span> b</code><span class="where">between two lines</span></td><td>induct</td><td>potential</td><td class="n">27</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>a <span data-t="integration">^</span> b</code><span class="where">between two lines</span></td><td>project</td><td>ascension</td><td class="n">1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>a <span data-t="subject">&amp;</span> b</code><span class="where">between two lines</span></td><td>subject</td><td>subject</td><td class="n">1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>a <span data-t="perspective">@</span> b</code><span class="where">between two lines</span></td><td>perspective</td><td>perspective</td><td class="n">0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code><span data-t="b-direction">{</span>a ~&gt; b<span data-t="b-direction">}</span></code><span class="where">inside a body</span></td><td>project</td><td>potential</td><td class="n">10</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code><span data-t="b-direction">{</span>a; b<span data-t="b-direction">}</span></code><span class="where">inside a body</span></td><td>ordinal</td><td></td><td class="n">69</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code><span data-t="b-direction">{</span>a, b<span data-t="b-direction">}</span></code><span class="where">inside a body</span></td><td>common</td><td></td><td class="n">38</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code><span data-t="b-direction">{</span>a.b<span data-t="b-direction">}</span></code><span class="where">inside a body</span></td><td>ident</td><td>ground</td><td class="n">686</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <h3 id="names">Where the names part</h3>
  <p>Each is open unless it says it is settled. Position may be the whole answer: a mark in front, between, and inside a body need not read alike.</p>
  <ol class="questions">
    <li><em>project</em> names two forms: <code>a ^ b</code> between two lines (1 on the shelf) and <code>{a ~&gt; b}</code> inside a body (10). The mark <code>^</code> is called ascension, and <code>~</code> potential.</li>
    <li><strong>Settled.</strong> <em>lens</em> used to name two things. <code>@</code> is an operation of perspective regarding its operand, so <code>viewer@subject</code> (9 on the shelf) reads the subject from the viewer's position, and the slot reader calls an <code>@</code> join perspective; the <code>[ ]</code> brace keeps the lens you are using (1058).</li>
    <li><code>~</code> reads three ways by where it sits: potential in front of a word (9), induct between two lines (27), and the start of a movement inside a body (10).</li>
    <li>The dot: to the parser <code>software.art.practice</code> is one word; the search reads three parts. 686 bodies on the shelf are written this way, and <code>.</code> on its own is called ground.</li>
    <li>A charge with nothing to ride: 17 lines open with a valence word and no other word, as <code>bane[bone]{limit}</code> does, so the reader takes the valence word as the charge and finds no word. The workbench reads the five as modifiers on a word.</li>
  </ol>
</section>
